{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the day HaShem spoke, etc.</b> Meaning: what you see, that Aaron was equal to Moses in all the details of this matter, was because <b>on the day that [HaShem] spoke</b>, etc., <b>in the land of Egypt.</b> That is, to exclude the first speaking that was in the wilderness, where HaShem said to him (Exodus 4:14) ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ״ etc., for there the verse had not yet equalized Aaron to Moses in the commission of this commandment, until HaShem spoke with Moses in the land of Egypt — and this is what it says: <b>on the day [He spoke]</b>, etc., <b>in the land of Egypt.</b>" "And the verse returned to state the manner of the speaking that HaShem spoke with Moses in the land of Egypt, namely: <b>And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying: I am HaShem. Speak to Pharaoh</b>, etc. The meaning of ״לֵאמֹר״ is that He was particular to say to him ״אֲנִי ה׳״, and the intention in this is that you should rely on My words and go on My mission and you yourself shall speak to Pharaoh all that, etc. And you will find that this speaking of ״אֲנִי ה׳״ was in the land of Egypt, as it is written (Exodus 6:2) ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״ — this was in Egypt, and He told him that he himself should go and speak to Pharaoh, not Aaron. And although HaShem had already joined Aaron with him, behold I explained well there in its place that Aaron would speak to the people, but to Pharaoh HaShem still desired that Moses speak, and He reassured him by saying <b>I am HaShem</b>, and you need not worry about anything. But Moses did not wish to do so, and he returned and protested: <b>Behold, I am of uncircumcised lips, and how will Pharaoh hear me?</b> Behold, the verse was precise in stating that the protest of uncircumcised lips was specifically regarding Pharaoh, since Aaron does not speak until now except to the people, as we have written. Then HaShem said to Moses: <b>See</b>, etc., <b>and Aaron your brother shall speak to Pharaoh</b> — meaning, also to Pharaoh, in addition to the people. And from now on Aaron and Moses were equalized in this commandment. And therefore you will find that HaShem did not say to Moses in the wilderness, when He joined Aaron with him, all these preparations that are stated here. And this should be a sign for you that they are new things, as mentioned for the reason stated." "<b>And behold,</b> HaShem has shown us that HaShem was particular about the matter that Moses did not do, for so much did HaShem exert Himself with Moses to have him do everything himself. And perhaps for this reason the verse said <b>וַיְהִי</b> in the language of distress, for the matter indicates that there would have been a great advantage if Moses had done everything, and the proof is from HaShem's exertion with him. Accordingly, He said ״וַיְהִי״ over the loss of that advantage." "<b>And it is possible</b> that for this the arranger of the parashiyyot intended to place this verse ״<b>וַיְהִי</b> on the day״ in the preceding parashah; and according to the truth it is connected with what follows below, as we have explained — rather, he intended to say that the distress alluded to in the word ״וַיְהִי״ is over performing the commandment through both of them together. And perhaps if Moses had performed this commandment alone, his strength would have been fortified and he would have entered the land, and the Israelites would not have experienced the hardships in the wilderness. Also, if he had entered the land he would have built the Temple and it would have stood for all time. Or perhaps it is for the lack of Moses that the language of distress was used." "<b>Further,</b> he intends [to explain] what [the reason is] that he interrupted the sequence, to say that all that is stated ״these are they whom HaShem spoke [to]״, etc., ״these are they who speak״, etc. — that they are equal in the commandment, the two of them together — this was <b>on the day HaShem spoke in the land of Egypt</b>, etc. But beforehand, although HaShem had said ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן״, etc., they were not equal in the commandment, for Moses was the one who performed the entire mission to Pharaoh. Not so now, when Aaron alone shall speak to Pharaoh, as He said: <b>he shall be your spokesman</b>, etc., <b>[he] shall speak to Pharaoh</b>:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:30.908707931Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}